+{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdMZezn9QrPQ3LiDzYeNq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013KodjVhNBhqgQvbr7P3n46","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/workspace/README.md","content":"# Reactive Spreadsheet Engine\n\nA spreadsheet engine as a TypeScript library. Cells hold values or formulas, and\nchanging a cell automatically recomputes everything that depends on it.\n\n```ts\nimport { Spreadsheet } from './src/index.js';\n\nconst sheet = new Spreadsheet();\nsheet.set('A1', 10);\nsheet.set('A2', 20);\nsheet.set('A3', '=A1+A2');\nsheet.get('A3');        // 30\nsheet.getDisplay('A3'); // \"30\"\n\nsheet.set('A1', 50);\nsheet.get('A3');        // 70 — recomputed automatically\n\nsheet.set('B1', '=SUM(A1:A2)');          // 70\nsheet.set('C1', '=IF(A1>30, \"big\", \"small\")'); // \"big\"\n```\n\n## API\n\n| Method | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| `set(cell, value)` | Store a value, or a formula when the string starts with `=`. |\n| `get(cell)` | Computed value, or `null` when the cell is empty. |\n| `getDisplay(cell)` | The string a spreadsheet would show. |\n| `getCells()` | Every non-empty cell and its value, in reading order. |\n| `clear(cell)` / `clearAll()` | Empty one cell, or the whole sheet. |\n| `getRaw(cell)` | The original input, formula text included. |\n| `isFormula(cell)` / `hasError(cell)` | Inspect a cell's kind. |\n| `getPrecedents(cell)` / `getDependents(cell)` | Direct edges of the dependency graph. |\n\nSupported: `+ - * /` with parentheses and unary signs, comparisons\n(`> < >= <= = <>`), references (`A1`, `AA100`), ranges (`A1:A5`, `A1:C1`,\n`A1:C3`), and `SUM`, `AVERAGE`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `COUNT`, `IF`.\n\n## How it works\n\nEach formula is parsed once into an AST, and the cells it reads are recorded as\nedges in a dependency graph. `set` then does three things:\n\n1. **Rejects cycles** before touching any state, so a failed `set` is a complete\n   no-op. The check runs two searches at once — forward from the cells the new\n   formula reads, backward from the cell being written — and stops as soon as\n   either runs dry. Either search alone would be correct but degrades on a\n   different shape of sheet; together they keep both linear.\n2. **Rewires the graph**, dropping edges from the formula being replaced.\n3. **Recomputes** the changed cell and its transitive dependents in topological\n   order (Kahn's algorithm over just that sub-graph), so every formula sees\n   up-to-date inputs and each cell is evaluated exactly once — no repeated work\n   when a diamond of dependencies reconverges.\n\n## Semantics\n\nThe spec left some behaviour open. The choices below follow Excel and Google\nSheets, and each is covered by tests.\n\n**Errors come in two kinds.** Structurally invalid input *throws* and leaves the\nsheet unchanged: a bad reference (`InvalidReferenceError`), an unparsable\nformula (`FormulaSyntaxError`), or a cycle (`CircularReferenceError`, which\ncarries the offending chain). A well-formed formula that cannot produce a value\ninstead yields an error *value* that propagates to dependents, readable as its\ncode — `#DIV/0!`, `#VALUE!`, `#NAME?`, `#NUM!`. Use `hasError` to distinguish a\nreal error from text that happens to look like one.\n\n**Display is not stringification** — that is why `getDisplay` exists next to\n`get`. Booleans render as `TRUE`/`FALSE`, empty cells as `''`, errors as their\ncode, and numbers at 15 significant digits, so `=0.1+0.2` shows `0.3` rather\nthan `0.30000000000000004`. `get` returns the raw unrounded value.\n\n**Coercion.** Empty cells read as `0` in arithmetic and as the neutral value of\nwhatever they are compared against. Booleans count as `1`/`0`. Text is converted\nonly if it is decimal notation, so `\"0x10\"` stays text. Aggregates skip text and\nbooleans found *inside a range* but convert values passed *directly*, so\n`SUM(A1:A5)` ignores a stray label while `SUM(1, TRUE)` is `2`. Text compares\ncase-insensitively, and mixed types order as number < text < boolean.\n\n**`IF` is lazy** — only the branch it takes is evaluated, so\n`=IF(A1=0, 0, 1/A1)` is safe when `A1` is zero. Its third argument is optional\nand defaults to `FALSE`.\n\n**Values passed to `set` keep their type**: `set('A1', '42')` stores the text\n`\"42\"`, not the number. Pass a number for a number. `set(cell, '')` clears.\n\n**Limits**, chosen to turn resource exhaustion into clean errors rather than a\ncrash: columns up to `ZZZ`, rows up to 1,048,576, one million cells per range,\nand an expression tree up to 1024 levels deep.\n\n## Development\n\n```sh\nnpm test      # vitest\nnpm run build # tsc\nnpm run lint  # eslint\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":725,"cache_read_input_tokens":113646,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":725},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ec19702f-ab51-4df4-bf38-5eeb7a393dd5","uuid":"2ad66ab7-b0be-4260-81ce-c3728b51f141","request_id":"req_011CdMZey5x1wtGPFc5wCzSq"}
